      ":question/id": "44c2d353-4676-59f2-bb10-c3adb87b8fbd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given $f(x)=x^{3}-x$ and $x\\ne 0$, calculate the integral.\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{f(x)}{x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+C.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{f(x)}{x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\frac{x^{3}-x}{x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{x^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x-\\int x^{-2}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{x}{1}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{x^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\ &=x+\\frac{1}{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe blank excludes the constant $C$, which is already supplied.",
